# FastAPI patterns

Narrows `python`'s stack-agnostic design guidance (and its own
`asyncio`/resource patterns) to FastAPI's own request lifecycle: dependency
injection, async vs. sync path operations, response modeling, and
background work. Applies only to FastAPI project files.

## Async vs. sync path operations

- Declare a path operation `async def` only when its body actually awaits
  something async (an async DB driver, `httpx.AsyncClient`, another
  awaitable). An `async def` path operation runs directly on the event
  loop, so a blocking call inside it (a sync DB driver, `requests.get`,
  `time.sleep`, blocking file I/O, a CPU-bound loop) stalls every other
  in-flight request on that worker.
- Declare a path operation with plain `def` when its body calls a blocking
  library with no async equivalent. FastAPI runs a `def` path operation in
  an external threadpool automatically — this is current, documented
  behavior, not a workaround — so a blocking call inside it does not block
  the event loop.
- The same rule applies to dependencies: a `def` dependency runs in the
  threadpool, an `async def` dependency runs on the loop and must not
  block. FastAPI accepts either kind of dependency under either kind of
  path operation (an `async def` dependency under a `def` path operation
  and vice versa), so choose per-dependency based on what its own body
  does, not to match the path operation's own declaration.
- For a compute-only path operation with no I/O at all, prefer `async def`
  — it avoids the threadpool's scheduling overhead for work that was never
  going to block anything.

## Dependency injection

- Use `Depends(...)` (or `Security(...)` for an auth dependency that also
  carries OAuth2 scopes) to inject anything a path operation needs but
  should not construct itself: a DB session, the current authenticated
  user, pagination parameters, a feature flag. This is what makes each
  piece independently testable via FastAPI's `app.dependency_overrides`.
- Chain dependencies (a dependency that itself depends on another) instead
  of duplicating the same setup logic across sibling dependencies — FastAPI
  caches a dependency's result per request by default, so a shared
  sub-dependency runs once even when several path-level dependencies need
  it.
- Use `yield` in a dependency that owns a resource needing teardown (a DB
  session, a lock, an open connection); the code after `yield` runs after
  the response is sent, even when the path operation raised — this is
  FastAPI's own resource-lifecycle pattern for a request-scoped resource,
  not a manual `try`/`finally` bolted onto a plain-return dependency.

## Request/response modeling

- Model every request body as a Pydantic `BaseModel`, never as a raw
  `dict`/`Any` parameter — FastAPI validates the incoming JSON against the
  model before the path operation body runs, which is the framework's own
  first line of defense against malformed or malicious input (see
  `rules/security.mdc`).
- Use `field_validator`/`model_validator` (Pydantic v2) for a constraint
  that spans multiple fields or needs custom logic beyond `Field(...)`'s
  declarative constraints (`gt`, `max_length`, `pattern`), not a manual
  `if`-check inside the path operation body after the model has already
  validated.
- Declare `response_model` (or a return-type annotation FastAPI can infer
  one from) so the response is filtered to the declared fields even when
  the object returned from the path operation carries more — this is a
  correctness property, not just documentation: it is what keeps an
  internal-only field out of the response body.

## Background work

- Use `BackgroundTasks` (injected the same way as any other dependency)
  for work that must run after the response is sent but is short, best-
  effort, and does not need retries, a result, or cross-process durability
  (writing a log line, sending a best-effort notification).
- Reach for a real task queue (Celery, arq, a message broker) instead of
  `BackgroundTasks` once the work needs retries, visibility into failure,
  distribution across multiple worker processes, or survival past the
  serving process' lifetime — `BackgroundTasks` runs in-process and is lost
  if the process restarts before it completes.

## OpenAPI and schema

- Let FastAPI generate the OpenAPI schema from type hints, `response_model`,
  and `Field(...)` constraints rather than hand-maintaining a separate
  OpenAPI YAML/JSON file alongside the code — the generated schema is what
  keeps documentation and implementation from drifting apart.
- Use `tags=[...]` on each `APIRouter`/path operation and a `summary`/
  `description` (or a docstring, which FastAPI uses as the description)
  for anything beyond an obvious CRUD endpoint, so the generated docs stay
  usable as the API grows.

## Anti-patterns to flag, not introduce

- An `async def` path operation that calls a synchronous DB driver or
  `requests.get` directly — this silently degrades every concurrent request
  on that worker instead of raising an obvious error.
- Accepting the request body as `request: Request` and manually calling
  `await request.json()` to bypass Pydantic validation — this discards
  FastAPI's own validation, error responses, and OpenAPI schema generation
  for that endpoint.
- Using `BackgroundTasks` for work that must not be lost (payment capture,
  an email a user is relying on) instead of a real task queue with
  retries.
